    // The test fuzzes the newOwner address. We keep the admissible domain wide but exclude the zero address to avoid the trivial case where both contracts would set owner to zero (the reference would set owner to zero as newOwner==0). We also fuzz an additional arbitrary address param to ensure the fuzzer can explore.
    function testFuzz_property(address newOwner) public {
        // admissible domain: newOwner can be any address except address(0)
        vm.assume(newOwner != address(0));

        // The reference contract's initial owner is address(0). We perform the call as that owner.
        vm.prank(address(0));
        try c.transferOwnership(newOwner) {
            // call succeeded
        } catch {
            // If the call reverts, that would contradict the reference behaviour for admissible inputs
            // but transferOwnership with onlyOwner where msg.sender==owner (address(0)) should succeed.
        }

        // Exactly one assertion: after transferOwnership, the public owner getter equals the provided newOwner.
        // The correct contract C sets owner = newOwner; the mutant sets owner = address(0) after setting newOwner and will fail this for some newOwner values (indeed for all newOwner != address(0)).
        assertTrue(c.owner() == newOwner);
    }
}
